From: MastaG <mastag@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd: release the BC-250 GFX clock/voltage override across a link bring-up

Deferring OD commits around link training is not enough, because the damage
is not done by the commit. It is done by the override being *held*.

Isolated on hardware with cyan-skillfish-governor-smu stopped in both cases,
so the governor process was not a variable and no commit occurred during
either run -- the only difference was whether a ForceGfxVid override was in
effect:

  override held (1000MHz / 799mV)   -> KDE <-> gamescope switch loses sync
  override released (SMU defaults)  -> switch works

Supporting evidence from the same board: with the governor never started
since boot (nothing ever forced) the switch is 3/3 reliable; with the
governor running (override held) it is 0/3. Three consecutive failures had
no OD commit within 37s, 41s and 94s of the modeset, which is what rules out
commit timing -- and with it any deferral window, at any length. Forcing a
*higher* clock does not help either: pinning the governor's floor to 1500MHz,
the same clock the passing runs sat at, still failed. It is the held state
itself.

So take the override off for the bring-up and put it back afterwards.
cs_od_force_suspend() sends the same RequestGfxclk + UnforceGfxVid pair that
PP_OD_RESTORE_DEFAULT_TABLE + PP_OD_COMMIT_DPM_TABLE does -- that exact
sequence, issued by hand, is what was shown to fix a live failure -- and a
delayed work restores the user's settings once the window closes.

Local adaptation (toolkit): the release is gated on a valid user setting --
sclk inside the hardware range -- as well as a forced voltage. Without that
guard a zeroed cyan_skillfish_user_settings, which is the state whenever the
governor drives the clock through the SMU path (that path never writes these
fields), satisfied "vddc != MAGIC" and released on every commit: a real
clock/voltage transition each time, and a restore that could only send
RequestGfxclk(0) and fail ("Set sclk failed!"). The guard makes the release
inert when there is no override to release, which is the only safe reading of
"release the override".

Restoring from a timer rather than a matching "link is up" callback because
no such signal exists for a PCON: it trains its HDMI side autonomously and DC
never learns when it finished. Every modeset re-arms the timer, so
back-to-back modesets hold the release open instead of restoring between
them. amdgpu.cs_od_unforce_ms tunes the window (default 3000ms, 0 disables
the release and restores the previous behaviour).

An OD commit arriving while the release is open is refused, exactly as during
link training, but its settings are already recorded -- the restore work
applies whatever is current when it fires, so a commit is delayed rather than
lost.

The SMU is reached through a file-static pointer recorded once its tables are
up, because DC has no route to a struct smu_context. On any non-Cyan-Skillfish
AMD GPU that pointer is NULL and the call returns immediately, so the two DC
call sites are inert there.

smu_cmn_send_smc_msg*() takes its own mailbox mutex and never touches a DC
lock, so calling it from the link-training path is one-directional -- there is
no lock nesting of the kind the existing atomic-flag interlock was shaped to
avoid. Both call sites run in process context from the atomic commit tail.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
@@ -28,8 +28,10 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/jiffies.h>
 #include <linux/limits.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/topology.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #include "amdgpu.h"
 #include "amdgpu_smu.h"
@@ -142,6 +144,38 @@
 extern unsigned long cs_pcon_frl_defer_until;
 
 /*
+ * How long the forced GFX clock/voltage state stays released after a display
+ * link is brought up. See cs_od_force_suspend() below. 0 disables the release
+ * entirely, restoring the behaviour of a kernel without this patch.
+ */
+static uint cs_od_unforce_ms = 3000;
+module_param(cs_od_unforce_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(cs_od_unforce_ms,
+		 "BC-250: how long to release a forced GPU clock/voltage override after a display link is brought up, in ms (0 = never release)");
+
+/*
+ * The SMU this ASIC implementation is driving, recorded once its tables are
+ * up. Needed because the release below is requested from DC, which has no
+ * route to a struct smu_context. A single pointer rather than a per-device
+ * lookup: Cyan Skillfish is a one-GPU APU, and this whole file is already
+ * specific to it.
+ */
+static struct smu_context *cs_od_smu;
+
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cs_od_force_lock);
+static bool cs_od_force_suspended;
+static void cs_od_force_restore_work_fn(struct work_struct *work);
+static DECLARE_DELAYED_WORK(cs_od_force_restore_work, cs_od_force_restore_work_fn);
+
+/*
+ * Called from DC (link_dp_training.c and link_dpms.c), which declares it
+ * extern rather than sharing a header -- there is no header common to the
+ * display and powerplay trees that either would naturally include. Declared
+ * here too so the definition below has a visible prototype.
+ */
+void cs_od_force_suspend(void);
+
+/*
  * On an 8-core board, decode the metrics table the way the pre-patch SMU
  * firmware laid it out. Only needed on an older BIOS that unlocks the two
  * extra cores without carrying the 8-core metrics firmware patch; with that
@@ -411,7 +445,50 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	return smu_v11_0_init_smc_tables(smu);
+	ret = smu_v11_0_init_smc_tables(smu);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Only now is this smu usable for the OD release in link_dpms.c.
+	 * Published under the same lock the release and teardown take, so
+	 * there is one consistent owner of this pointer.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+	cs_od_smu = smu;
+	mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int cyan_skillfish_fini_smc_tables(struct smu_context *smu)
+{
+	bool was_ours;
+
+	/*
+	 * Drop the reference under the lock, so anything already inside
+	 * cs_od_force_suspend() or the restore work has either finished with
+	 * this smu or will now see NULL and send nothing.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+	was_ours = cs_od_smu == smu;
+	if (was_ours)
+		cs_od_smu = NULL;
+	mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+
+	if (was_ours) {
+		/*
+		 * Outside the lock: the work takes it, so cancelling while
+		 * holding it would deadlock against a restore already running.
+		 */
+		cancel_delayed_work_sync(&cs_od_force_restore_work);
+
+		mutex_lock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+		cs_od_force_suspended = false;
+		mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+	}
+
+	return smu_v11_0_fini_smc_tables(smu);
 }
 
 static int cyan_skillfish_refresh_metrics_table(struct smu_context *smu)
@@ -910,12 +987,146 @@
 	return sizeof(struct gpu_metrics_v2_2);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Push one GFX clock/voltage state to the SMU. Shared by the OD commit path
+ * and by the release/restore below so the two cannot drift apart. A vddc of
+ * CYAN_SKILLFISH_VDDC_MAGIC means "do not force a voltage", which is what
+ * PP_OD_RESTORE_DEFAULT_TABLE selects.
+ */
+static int cs_od_send_gfx_state(struct smu_context *smu, uint32_t sclk,
+				uint32_t vddc)
+{
+	uint32_t vid;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = smu_cmn_send_smc_msg_with_param(smu, SMU_MSG_RequestGfxclk,
+					      sclk, NULL);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Set sclk failed!\n");
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	if (vddc == CYAN_SKILLFISH_VDDC_MAGIC) {
+		ret = smu_cmn_send_smc_msg(smu, SMU_MSG_UnforceGfxVid, NULL);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Unforce vddc failed!\n");
+			return ret;
+		}
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * PMFW accepts SVI2 VID code, convert voltage to VID:
+		 * vid = (uint32_t)((1.55 - voltage) * 160.0 + 0.00001)
+		 */
+		vid = (1550 - vddc) * 160 / 1000;
+		ret = smu_cmn_send_smc_msg_with_param(smu, SMU_MSG_ForceGfxVid,
+						      vid, NULL);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Force vddc failed!\n");
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Release a forced GFX clock/voltage override for the duration of a display
+ * link bring-up, then put it back.
+ *
+ * Isolated on hardware: while the SMU is *holding* a ForceGfxVid override, a
+ * DP->HDMI 2.1 PCON fails to bring its HDMI side up on a mode change, and the
+ * sink loses sync. The same board with nothing forced switches modes
+ * reliably. Established with the governor stopped in both cases, so the only
+ * variable was the override itself:
+ *
+ *   force held (1000MHz/799mV)      -> mode switch loses sync
+ *   force released (SMU defaults)   -> mode switch works
+ *
+ * It is the held state, not the act of committing: three consecutive failures
+ * had no OD commit within 37s, 41s and 94s of the modeset. Deferring commits
+ * around link training -- what the interlock below does -- therefore cannot
+ * fix this on its own, and the forced state has to actually come off.
+ *
+ * Restoring is done from a delayed work rather than a matching "link is up"
+ * callback because no such signal exists for a PCON: it trains its HDMI side
+ * autonomously and DC never learns when it finished. Every modeset re-arms
+ * the timer, so back-to-back modesets hold the release open rather than
+ * restoring between them.
+ */
+void cs_od_force_suspend(void)
+{
+	struct smu_context *smu;
+	bool armed = false;
+
+	if (!cs_od_unforce_ms)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+	/*
+	 * Read inside the lock, not before it: cyan_skillfish_fini_smc_tables()
+	 * clears this under the same lock, so a teardown racing a modeset
+	 * cannot leave us sending mailbox messages to a device on its way out.
+	 */
+	smu = cs_od_smu;
+	if (!smu) {
+		mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (cs_od_force_suspended) {
+		/* Already released -- just hold it open a while longer. */
+		armed = true;
+	} else if (cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk >= CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MIN &&
+		   cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk <= CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MAX &&
+		   cyan_skillfish_user_settings.vddc != CYAN_SKILLFISH_VDDC_MAGIC) {
+		if (!cs_od_send_gfx_state(smu, cyan_skillfish_sclk_default,
+					  CYAN_SKILLFISH_VDDC_MAGIC)) {
+			WRITE_ONCE(cs_od_force_suspended, true);
+			armed = true;
+			if (cs_od_defer_debug)
+				dev_info(smu->adev->dev,
+					 "cs_od_defer: released forced GFX clock/voltage for link bring-up\n");
+		}
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+
+	if (armed)
+		mod_delayed_work(system_wq, &cs_od_force_restore_work,
+				 msecs_to_jiffies(cs_od_unforce_ms));
+}
+
+static void cs_od_force_restore_work_fn(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct smu_context *smu;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+	smu = cs_od_smu;
+	if (cs_od_force_suspended && smu) {
+		/*
+		 * Whatever the user's settings are *now* -- an OD commit that
+		 * arrived while the release was open updated them and was
+		 * refused, so this is where it lands.
+		 */
+		cs_od_send_gfx_state(smu, cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk,
+				     cyan_skillfish_user_settings.vddc);
+		if (cs_od_defer_debug)
+			dev_info(smu->adev->dev,
+				 "cs_od_defer: restored forced GFX clock/voltage after link bring-up\n");
+	}
+	/*
+	 * Cleared even if the restore failed: leaving it set would wedge the
+	 * override off permanently, and unforced is the safe state to be
+	 * stuck in.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(cs_od_force_suspended, false);
+	mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
+}
+
 static int cyan_skillfish_od_edit_dpm_table(struct smu_context *smu,
 					enum PP_OD_DPM_TABLE_COMMAND type,
 					long input[], uint32_t size)
 {
 	int ret = 0;
-	uint32_t vid;
 
 	switch (type) {
 	case PP_OD_EDIT_VDDC_CURVE:
@@ -972,6 +1181,20 @@
 			return -EBUSY;
 		}
 
+		/*
+		 * The override is currently released for a link bring-up.
+		 * Re-forcing here would defeat the whole point, so refuse --
+		 * the settings above have already been updated, and
+		 * cs_od_force_restore_work_fn() applies them when the window
+		 * closes.
+		 */
+		if (READ_ONCE(cs_od_force_suspended)) {
+			if (cs_od_defer_debug)
+				dev_info(smu->adev->dev,
+					 "cs_od_defer: deferring OD commit, GFX override released for link bring-up\n");
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+
 		if (cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk < CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MIN ||
 		    cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk > CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MAX) {
 			dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Invalid sclk! Valid sclk range: %uMHz - %uMhz\n",
@@ -987,31 +1210,11 @@
 			return -EINVAL;
 		}
 
-		ret = smu_cmn_send_smc_msg_with_param(smu, SMU_MSG_RequestGfxclk,
-					cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk, NULL);
-		if (ret) {
-			dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Set sclk failed!\n");
+		ret = cs_od_send_gfx_state(smu,
+					   cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk,
+					   cyan_skillfish_user_settings.vddc);
+		if (ret)
 			return ret;
-		}
-
